{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 71061cdd984bcc3e3758235d1250a0291ff84d47, against all 68 submitted artifacts, client README first. The three findings in review 17013617 are substantially addressed: signed receipt recovery bypasses sessions and genesis reads; answered receipts persist across local failures; native stream cancellation and error normalization work. Amendment 2 and the six earlier corrections remain intact.\n\nP2 — Fence recovered local steps against newer work (packages/cli/src/main.ts, claim completion and release completion; packages/cli/src/git.ts clearWorkspace). An answered journal stores the old receipt, but its completion runs again against whichever local state and working directory exist now. Independent diagnostics reproduce both cases. (1) Claim a lane and configure a workspace; release with a stable idempotency key, interrupted at act-answered; claim a different lane and configure its workspace in the same repository; retry the old release. The command succeeds and retains the new lane in config, yet deletes its credential file. There is exactly one remote release. (2) Interrupt an older claim at act-answered, finish a different claim, then recover the older claim. It silently replaces the selected newer lane with the older receipt's lane. This can break active work and direct subsequent default commands at the wrong lane. Persist the ownership and intended local transition with the journal, and condition recovery on that ownership/current version. A retained remote receipt must remain recoverable without fresh authority. If newer local work supersedes its side effects, finish receipt recovery without deleting or overwriting that work. Bind credential cleanup to the actual workspace/credential installed for the released lane, rather than the retry command's arbitrary current directory. Add interrupted and interleaved recovery tests, including recovery from another directory, plus the existing positive crash cases.\n\nArchitecture: direct signed resubmission respects retained idempotency, and prepared/answered journals correctly distinguish unknown transport outcomes from durable answers. Local effects now need the same fencing discipline as remote lane effects. The full client, CLI and MCP scope remains required; schedules impose no feature reduction.\nSecurity: credential redaction, authority-first unsigned retries, room-ID checks, own-property MCP dispatch, and normalized decoder failures remain effective in the reviewed code. Historical release recovery must not remove a newer workspace's authorization material.\nSimplification: the binary-only CLI package and standard stream transforms are useful simplifications. Keep receipt recovery centralized; implement a shared ownership check rather than command-specific guesses or extra session preflights.\n\nValidation: clean isolated exact-head install (337 packages), root strict typecheck; 86 client Node tests, 2 client workerd tests, 46 CLI tests, 66 MCP Node tests and 1 MCP workerd test passed; CLI build, bundled help and four-file dry package passed. Two independent CLI diagnostics pin the interleaved defects above; a structural RPC ByteStream cancellation diagnostic passes, as do the native lifecycle tests. No source changes were made in the builder checkout, and no live service was deployed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:497529e66fd433e9b46dbc7636542425aeaa8082","binding":"assigned","head":"71061cdd984bcc3e3758235d1250a0291ff84d47","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1f6e417893aaff733cc615ca9eeebb6a2a8dbb0f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3f934a98fa8efcc34c1695fee9b59e58568876e3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e9ef379871787dfaf0282b012a70fc5868af950d","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}